      "_id": "69ebc87f3a63baf205388f1e",
      "latePostId": "69e9dec37eb34a30e6b67a16",
      "content": "AI hallucination isn't a bug engineers forgot to patch — it's how language models fundamentally work 🤖\n\nWhy LLMs Hallucinate: The Mechanism\n\nWhat LLMs actually do:\n→ Given all preceding text, predict the most statistically likely next token\n→ That is the complete description of inference\n→ No fact lookup\n→ No verified knowledge base\n→ No truth-checking module\n→ Just: what token is most statistically consistent with training patterns?\n\nWhy this causes hallucination:\n→ A plausible-sounding false claim can match statistical patterns of confident text\n→ The model generates it — because statistically it fits\n→ The model has no internal signal: 'I know this' vs 'I'm generating a plausible completion'\n→ Both processes produce identical confident fluent prose\n→ Confidence is stylistic — not a reliability indicator\n\nMeasured rates (GPT-4-class models):\n→ Coding: ~2-5% error rate\n→ General factual: ~8-15%\n→ Medical citations (name paper authors, journals): ~20-30%\n→ Legal case citation: similar\n→ Same model confidence level regardless of domain accuracy\n\nWhy models sound confident when wrong:\n→ RLHF = fine-tuning on human preference ratings\n→ Humans rate confident, direct answers HIGHER than hedged, uncertain ones\n→ Model learns: confident-sounding = higher score = reinforced\n→ Calibrated uncertainty and fluent language pull in opposite directions\n→ We trained the confidence in\n\nChain-of-thought (o1/o3):\n→ Forces step-by-step reasoning before answering\n→ Reduces hallucination — model catches internal inconsistencies\n→ Doesn't eliminate it — the reasoning itself can hallucinate\n\nThe fundamental problem:\n→ 'Sound confident' and 'express appropriate uncertainty' are in tension\n→ Optimizing for one hurts the other\n→ Not purely technical — embedded in how we train these systems\n\n#aifacts #hallucination #llm #chatgpt #howaiworks #artificialintelligence #aiexplained #machinelearning #rlhf #promptengineering",
